{"name":"Four Leaf Clover","hex":"#738f5d","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"four","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["NATO & ICAO radiotelephony clear code (spelling-alphabet name) for the digit 4."]},{"part":"Numeral","definitions":["A numerical value equal to 4; the number following three and preceding five.","Describing a set or group with four elements."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/four"},{"term":"leaf","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The usually green and flat organ that represents the most prominent feature of most vegetative plants.","A foliage leaf or any of the many and often considerably different structures it can specialise into."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce leaves; put forth foliage.","To divide (a vegetable) into separate leaves."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leaf"},{"term":"clover","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of the genus Trifolium with leaves usually divided into three (rarely four) leaflets and with white or red flowers.","The second Lenormand card, representing hope, optimism and short-term luck."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clover"}],"entries":[{"title":"Four Leaf Clover (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Four Leaf Clover\" is a song by American folk-rock singer Abra Moore. Written by Moore and produced by Mitch Watkins, the song was released as Moore's debut single and as the lead single from her first studio album, Strangest Places (1997). The song became a hit in the United States, topping the Billboard Triple-A chart, and also reached the top 40 in Canada. The song's music video, directed by Nancy Bardawil, shows Moore performing the track outdoors and in several rooms. At the 40th Annual Grammy Awards, the song was nominated for Best Female Rock Vocal Performance."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/0f/Four_Leaf_Clover_Abra_Moore_cover.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_Leaf_Clover_(song)","wikidata":"Q124309607"},{"title":"Four-leaf clover","blocks":[{"text":"The four-leaf clover is a rare mutation of the common three-leaf clover that has four leaflets instead of three. According to tradition, such clovers bring good luck, a belief that dates back to at least the 17th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Four-leaf_Clover_Trifolium_repens_2.jpg/330px-Four-leaf_Clover_Trifolium_repens_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four-leaf_clover","wikidata":"Q756314"},{"title":"Leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A leaf is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata, the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll, which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Lisc_lipy.jpg/330px-Lisc_lipy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf","wikidata":"Q33971"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/0f/Four_Leaf_Clover_Abra_Moore_cover.png/250px-Four_Leaf_Clover_Abra_Moore_cover.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/0f/Four_Leaf_Clover_Abra_Moore_cover.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/0f/Four_Leaf_Clover_Abra_Moore_cover.png/250px-Four_Leaf_Clover_Abra_Moore_cover.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFour_Leaf_Clover_Abra_Moore_cover.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Four-leaf_Clover_Trifolium_repens_2.jpg/500px-Four-leaf_Clover_Trifolium_repens_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Four-leaf_Clover_Trifolium_repens_2.jpg/1280px-Four-leaf_Clover_Trifolium_repens_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Four-leaf_Clover_Trifolium_repens_2.jpg/500px-Four-leaf_Clover_Trifolium_repens_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"4-leaf white clover (Trifolium repens L.)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFour-leaf_Clover_Trifolium_repens_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Four_Leaf_Clover_%287258825680%29.jpg/500px-Four_Leaf_Clover_%287258825680%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Four_Leaf_Clover_%287258825680%29.jpg/1280px-Four_Leaf_Clover_%287258825680%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn